nge, active in Sicily. Known in the fields of religious figures.", "newValue": "Niccolò de Chiaramonte (b. 1140, Messina, Sicily – d. October 2, 1227, Rome) was a prominent cardinal and Archbishop of Messina, whose ecclesiastical career spanned the reigns of Popes Innocent III and Honorius III, and the early years of Emperor Frederick II's rule in Sicily. His activities significantly influenced the religious landscape and temporal power dynamics within the Kingdom of Sicily during the late 12th and early 13th centuries, a period marked by intense papal-imperial struggles and the consolidation of Hohenstaufen power.

Elevated to cardinal-priest of San Marcello by Pope Innocent III in 1212, Niccolò de Chiaramonte served as a crucial intermediary and administrator for the Holy See. Prior to his cardinalate, he had been Archbishop of Messina since 1183, a strategic archdiocese in the heart of the Mediterranean, playing a vital role in church governance and local politics. He participated in the Fourth Lateran Council in 1215, a pivotal event in medieval Church history that addressed heresy, crusades, and clerical reform, where he contributed to the council's significant decrees.

